<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paraná, Entre Ríos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paraná is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entre-rios-province">Entre Ríos Province</a>, on the east bank of the Paraná river opposite Santa Fe. From 1853 to 1861 it was the capital of the Argentine Confederation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a>, while the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> remained separate. It is now a regional centre of trade and administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parana-entre-rios.jpg" alt="Paraná, Entre Ríos" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ANDY ABIR ALAN · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panor%C3%A1mica_de_Paran%C3%A1,_Entre_R%C3%ADos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Paraná stands on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-river">Paraná River</a>, opposite the city of Santa Fe, capital of the neighbouring Santa Fe Province. Its original name was Bajada, meaning landing. The climate is Pampean, classed as humid subtropical, with an average annual temperature of 18 °C. Winters have mild days and very cold nights, with occasional frosts, while summers are hot and bring most of the rain, about 1,069 millimetres in a year. The city and the whole province lie in an area of high tornado risk, particularly in spring and summer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1730 inhabitants of Santa Fe settled on the other shore of the river and called the place Baxada del Paraná. The first settlers had been forced out of Santa Fe by raiding Payaguá and Muños peoples. They settled in what is now the Square of the first of May, where they built a fort, other defences and a series of huts. Their numbers eventually became stable enough for them to move from defence to offence, and they almost wiped out both peoples. In the middle of the 19th century the city served for some years as the capital of the Argentine Confederation, until 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Paraná is the seat of the provincial government of Entre Ríos and an important river port for the transshipment of cereals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, fish and lumber from the surrounding region. Its industries include the manufacture of cement, furniture and ceramics. The city centre combines colonial churches with buildings in European styles such as the 3 de Febrero Theatre and the Government House, and the Cathedral blends several architectural styles. The Hernandarias Subfluvial Tunnel, inaugurated in 1969, links the city to Santa Fe beneath the river. Its main sports clubs include the basketball team Atlético Echagüe and the football clubs Patronato and Atlético Paraná.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paraná, Entre Ríos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Justo José de Urquiza defeated him at Caseros in 1852, a congress adopted the Constitution of 1853, still in force, and the government sat at <b>Paraná</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/argentine-confederation">Argentine Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>Buenos Aires kept the revenues of its port, the country's main source of income, while the Argentine Confederation governed from <b>Paraná</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-buenos-aires">State of Buenos Aires</a></li></ul></section>
